   "lessons": [
    "Every skill's first line asks Claude Code to run a config helper; without it, read project.yaml by hand and fall back to config-resolution.md's table. Rigor minimal expands to solo review, so /start must not write review_mode, or it pins it.",
    "Stories that each pass their criteria can still add up to a game nobody can lose. Get a balance probe (here, the title screen's self-playing keeper run over 16 seeds) working as soon as the core loop exists, not after the last story.",
    "Run-and-observe needs a way to reach a state: a small debug hook (set frost, time, a stall) is the browser's equivalent of the skill's launch arguments. Keep the hit pause and other cosmetics out of step() so snapshots stay deterministic.",
    "Test tooling lies too. A guessed tap position and a balance bot that could never hit the crank both looked like game bugs; measure positions in the page, and check the probe before blaming the game.",
    "At minimal, /story-done writes only the story file, so EPIC.md's status table goes stale by design; the story files and session logs are the record."
   ],
   "deviations": "No engine (the brief's choice): /setup-engine was not run, the engine and commands were added to project.yaml by hand, and every story's engine risk reads NOT ASSESSED. None of the pack's hooks or scripts were run (config helper, stamps, artifact checks); each was done by hand and the logs say so, the evidence stamps being plain sha256 lists. Evidence lives in studio/production/qa/evidence/ (the studio's own path, as the operating notes asked) rather than studio/evidence/. After the playtest the human named an eighth story (tuning), and two bugs were filed and fixed outside any story. The binder itself is page code, not a studio story. The code root is the showcase folder, not studio/src.",
   "browser_support": "any modern browser (Canvas 2D and WebAudio); opens from file:// too",
   "checked": "At 1440×900 and 390×844 (plus 1280×720, 1920×1080 and 2560×1080): zero console errors, zero failed requests, no sideways scroll. Full nights played end to end with real input: desktop by clicks and keys, phone by taps and long-presses on WORK, from the title through two fuel runs and three stalls to the end screen, Play again and pause. That was three desktop nights (3,000 → compromised → 2,733 across tuning and bug fixes) and one phone night (3,000 before tuning); after the final typography pass, one more full night per size on the shipped build: desktop dawn 2,704, phone dawn 2,944, both with zero errors. Every story was run, looked at and kept as WebP evidence (all under 60 KB). Determinism (same seed and inputs give the same snapshot), the keyboard-only menus, focus, M mute stored, reduce flashing on under prefers-reduced-motion, auto-pause on blur and hidden, no AudioContext before a gesture, the iframe preview (muted demo night, binder and label hidden) and opening from file:// were each checked by probe. Balance was run over 16 seeds at four keeper skills; render cost measured at 6.5 ms (1440) and 3.4 ms (390) in software rendering.",
   "prompt": "Use Claude Code Game Studios (Donchitos/Claude-Code-Game-Studios) in its lightest mode to make a small, finished browser game, and publish the studio's paperwork next to it. The page is the game plus a \"studio binder\" that shows how the studio made it.\n\nRun the studio's `minimal` jam flow, playing both the studio and its one human (the person who answers its questions):\n1. `/start`: the answers are \"vague concept\", engine **none: a plain web page with Canvas 2D** (the studio expects Godot, Unity or Unreal; record this choice and why), `modes.rigor: minimal`, `review_mode: solo`.\n2. `/brainstorm night shift at a seed vault during a storm`: write the 2 or 3 one-line concepts it asks for, pick one as the human (say why), and fill the one-page brief from its `game-brief.md` template: working title, pitch, core loop, goal and fail state, an MVP of 7 items or fewer.\n3. `/create-stories`: turn the MVP into stories with acceptance criteria, as the skill describes for `minimal`.\n4. `/dev-story` ↔ `/story-done` for every story, in the brief's build order. Follow the studio's run-and-observe rule: a story that changes what the player sees is not done until the game was run, looked at and a screenshot kept as evidence.\n5. `/playtest-report` after a real scripted playtest, `/gate-check` for release, and a short `/retrospective`.\n\nKeep the documents the studio writes in `studio/` inside your folder (Markdown, following the skills' templates and file names as closely as a one-page web game allows), and the evidence screenshots as small WebP files in `studio/evidence/` (under 60 KB each). Don't fake any of it: every document must be one you actually wrote while building, in order.\n\nThe page: the game first, full width, playable at once. Below it (or behind a binder tab on phones) the binder, styled as a studio production folder: the three pitches with the chosen one marked, the brief, the story cards with their status and evidence thumbnails, the playtest report, the gate verdicts as stamps, and the retrospective. Render the Markdown into the page at build time (embed it; the page must also open from disk), with a link to each raw file.\n\nThe game itself: whatever the studio chose, but it must be a one-screen 2D canvas game with 3 to 5 minute runs, keyboard and touch controls, and the house rules' test hooks.\n\nLook: the game gets its own art direction from the brief; the binder looks like real studio paperwork (manila, carbon copies, typed labels, rubber stamps) without being a pastiche. Different type for game and binder.",
   "steps": [
    "Install Claude Code (`npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code`) and sign in.",
    "Get the Claude Code Game Studios skills with the commands in **Install**, and read their `SKILL.md` files before you use them.",
    "Give the agent a way to play its own game: Playwright MCP (`claude mcp add playwright npx @playwright/mcp@latest`), or `node scripts/inspect.mjs` from this repo, whose `--steps` option plays a scripted round with real key presses, clicks and taps and screenshots it.",
    "Make an empty folder, open a terminal in it and run `claude`.",
    "Paste the full prompt from **Prompt** (the brief followed by the house rules). Outside this repo, drop the line about `shared/tag.js`; it only adds this collection's corner label.",
    "Let it build, then ask it to play a full round, from the title screen to the end screen, at desktop and phone sizes, look at the screenshots and fix what's off. Ask for the `window.__game` test hooks early: they let it replay a seed and check that inputs really change the game.",
    "Expect a different game from the one here: the same prompt gives a new design each run. Read **Notes** for the snags this build hit."
   ],
